          "text": "**Observations:**\n- **Frame usage.** Row 0 weights are all tiny (about ±0.035, i.e. still at initialization), so that row is essentially always blank. Row 1 is different: inputs 32–51 (columns 4–23) carry clearly trained weights of 0.1–0.2. Row 2 has even larger weights, up to about 0.4 around columns 10–11. So the input content regularly reaches the second row from the top.\n- **Edge pixels.** Many rows have unusually large weights (magnitude 0.2–0.38) in single lines. These appear to fall at the extreme left and right columns (0 and 27) for most of the image height, though I did not count positions exactly. If so, the objects often span the full width of the frame.\n- **Bottom rows.** Rows 25–26 also carry trained weights of 0.1–0.3. The content therefore extends nearly to the bottom edge.\n- **Shape of the weight maps.** The structure is broad and blob-like, roughly symmetric left/right. Some units show long vertical bands of same-sign weights across the central columns (e.g. units 2, 6 and 15 have long runs of negative weights in middle columns). That looks like silhouette or region detection rather than thin-stroke detection.\n- **Output layer.** Each hidden unit feeds several classes with mixed signs. A few units drive groups of classes together:\n  - unit 2 → classes 4 and 6;\n  - unit 12 → classes 6, 5 and 0;\n  - unit 9 → classes 7 and 1;\n  - unit 14 → classes 7, 0 and 5.\n  This would fit overlapping, visually similar categories, but it is only a weak hint.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- **MNIST is unlikely.** Its digits sit inside a roughly 20×20 box centred in the 28×28 frame. Border rows and columns (rows 0–3 and 24–27, columns 0–3 and 24–27) are almost always zero, so their weights would stay near initialization. Here, row 1, rows 25–26 and possibly the left/right edge columns have clearly trained weights. EMNIST digits behave similarly.\n- **Fashion-MNIST fits.** Its centred, full-frame silhouettes touch the edges:\n  - bags, sneakers, sand